      Loop structure:                    Rolled loop (unroll factor 1: 2 float ops in body, 1 branch)

Multi-Run Measurement Stability (Shared DGX Cluster):
  Run 1: MiniPTX 0.4721 ms vs CUDA -O3 0.4209 ms -> Ratio: 1.122x
  Run 2: MiniPTX 0.4424 ms vs CUDA -O3 0.3948 ms -> Ratio: 1.121x
  Run 3: MiniPTX 0.4874 ms vs CUDA -O3 0.4342 ms -> Ratio: 1.123x

Empirical Conclusions:
  1. Hardware FMA Fusion Root Cause:
     MiniPTX generates explicit mul.rn.f32 and add.rn.f32 (two dependent instructions:
     FMUL followed by FADD). nvcc -O3 contracts these by default into a single FFMA.
     When nvcc is compiled with --fmad=false, it also emits FMUL + FADD and its runtime
     matches MiniPTX within measurement noise (0.4874 ms vs 0.4884 ms, 0.998x).
     FMA contraction alone provides an 11.11% speedup, accounting for the entire gap.
  2. Loop Unrolling Ruled Out:
     All three kernels preserve a rolled loop with 1 backward branch and a single arithmetic
     sequence in the loop body. This rules out unrolling differences as a factor. (Note:
     the rolled/unrolled check is a heuristic based on instruction and branch counts).
  3. Static SASS Count Does Not Predict Performance:
     MiniPTX has 24 SASS instructions while no-FMA CUDA has 32, yet both achieve identical
     runtime (~0.487 ms - 0.488 ms). Static instruction counts do not correlate with runtime
     when loops dictate dynamic execution cycles.
